><text color="#9643FC" creationdate="D:20251016111704+10'00'" flags="print,nozoom,norotate" inreplyto="6570b6aa-78da-44fa-9732-a6eee8de470d" date="D:20251016111704+10'00'" name="2777110c-f0f9-4cb5-892f-5c5aa961c936" icon="Comment" page="9" rect="41.102570,721.891479,65.102570,745.891479" subject="Sticky Note" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#221E1F;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>The increase in RMR observed&#xD;was small (50–75 kcal·d</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-8.7pt;text-align:left;color:#221E1F;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>−1</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#221E1F;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>) and is unlikely to have clinically&#xD;significant implications for an athlete’s total daily energy requirements.&#xD;However, RMR represents only one component&#xD;of daily energy requirements, and physiological adaptations&#xD;that occur with altitude may alter other components that contribute&#xD;to daily energy needs. Given the downward trend in&#xD;RMR that was seen upon return to sea level, care should be&#xD;taken when measuring and interpreting the RMR of athletes&#xD;immediately post-altitude.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="9" rect="584.956970,653.891479,768.956970,745.891479"
/></text
